{"title":"Infinity","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Infinity</b> (denoted by the symbol <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/7/b671f1bb7e4ee86584347d5d22f1dc8abdb5bef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"9\" />) is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Math\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Math\">mathematical</a> concept that describes something that has no bounds. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/c/1dc1c0119a604b91be9142370dc3159b6a9bbcb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tau$\" width=\"9\" height=\"8\" /> have a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite\" title=\"Infinite\">infinite</a> number of digits because they never end. The first use of infinity was during Ancient Greek by Anaximander who referred to it as \"apeiron\". The symbol, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/7/b671f1bb7e4ee86584347d5d22f1dc8abdb5bef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"9\" /> was first used by English <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematician\" title=\"Mathematician\">mathematician</a> John Wallis in 1655.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Definition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Definition</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Infinite_sums\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Infinite sums</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Operations_with_infinity\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Operations with infinity</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition\">Definition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Definition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Infinity is the cardinality of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b43768913fd5dd350a34daf1051839ef19642053.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb Z$\" width=\"11\" height=\"12\" />, the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">set</a> of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integers</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Infinite_sums\">Infinite sums</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Infinite sums\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A sum works the same way. Certain sums equate to infinity, such as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/e/24e32ca1e54c2c5e324260779e14953a3d83261b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{i=3}^{\\infty}{(2i - 1)}\\]\" width=\"87\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Operations_with_infinity\">Operations with infinity</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Operations with infinity\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Some rules involving operations with infinity are as follows:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/2/cb2dfb17662882e7803d0df8662d938828c1c8f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\infty} + x  = {\\pm}{\\infty}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"106\" height=\"13\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/5/9352a83384ac5f4dec53453c3195027b2415040f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\infty}\\cdot{x} = {\\infty}\\quad\\forall x\\in\\mathbb Z_+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"168\" height=\"18\" /></li></ul>\n<p>We can also take the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Limit\" title=\"Limit\">limit</a> of division by infinity as follows:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/7/c4727a560d9fd86c5dcb8a2bec9a90724f29c2e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}1/x =0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"101\" height=\"28\" /></li></ul>\n<p>Note that many standard laws of arithmetic on the set of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complex_number\" title=\"Complex number\">complex numbers</a> do not apply to infinity.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite\" title=\"Infinite\">Infinite</a>\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Infinity&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018091316\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.03